# Design System

- Reuse shared components for controls, navigation, dialogs, fields, feedback, imagery, and loading. Extend the existing variant when multiple features share the need.
- Use semantic theme tokens for color, typography, spacing, and elevation. Keep palette literals in theme definitions. Follow the project's icon system and verify supported light, dark, and system appearances.
- Localize user-facing copy, including accessible labels, tooltips, error states, menus, and native-facing text where applicable. Keep supported locale keys aligned. Use locale-aware formatting for dates, numbers, and plurals.
- Use directional layout properties and mirror only appropriate controls for RTL. Render user-authored text with its own bidirectional direction and suitable script-aware fonts and fallbacks. Verify mixed Arabic and Latin text, including in editable fields.
- Use available space and content constraints to drive layout. Allow text to grow at system text scales and with long translations. Avoid fixed text containers that clip or hide essential content.
- Give controls accessible names, roles, and states. Preserve visible focus, logical traversal, and sufficient contrast. Pair status colors and icons with text or semantics. Honor reduced-motion settings and test screen-reader navigation for affected flows.
- Use stable placeholders and match image decoding and cache policy to intended display sizes. Keep interactive overlays reachable without obscuring underlying controls.
